@emmrk @vlvkobal wouldnt be better to split `pluginsd_worker_thread` into several funcs? And get rid of both nested if/else and goto's?  

> wouldnt be better to split `pluginsd_worker_thread` into several funcs

i meant move some logic from the func into separate func.

generic example,just to show what i mean,  i haven't analyzed `plugins_d.c`

```c

if (code != 0) {
  handleFailure(cd)
} else {
  handleSuccess(cd)
}

void handleFailure(struct plugind *cd) {
  if(likely(!cd->successful_collections)) {
    do something...
    return
  }

  if(likely(cd->serial_failures <= 10)) {
    do something...
    return
  }

  do something...

}

```

I am in favour of using `goto` in C code when it makes things clearer, keeps code shorter, cuts down nesting as shown in [dup_mm()](https://lxr.missinglinkelectronics.com/linux/kernel/fork.c#L1309). In this example the coder saves a ton of duplicated cleanup code and nesting. `goto` instructions are typically used as an exception handling substitute in C.

In this PR I don't find the new code to be more readable than the old code. The new code is 63 lines long when it used to be 42 lines, and the following comments were removed for no good reason:
```
 // nothing collected - disable it

 // we have collected something

 // we have collected nothing so far
```
Adding the comments back would make the new code 69 lines versus 42 lines of the old code.

Moreover, I find this from the old code:
```
 if(code != 0) {
...
 else {
```
more readable than this from the new code:
```
 if (likely(code == 0)) {
...
 if(code != 0) {
```

Ideally I'd keep the best of the 2 approaches, something like this:
```
 if (likely(code == 0)) {
...
else {
```
